The hadronic form factors $f_{T}$, $f_{P}$, and $f_{S}$ involved in the $B_{s}^{0}\rightarrow D_{s}^{*-}\mu^{+}\nu_{\mu}$ semi-leptonic decay are evaluated utilizing the Light-Cone Sum Rule approach. By analyzing the dependence of related quantities on the momentum transfer squared, it is determined that $f_{T}$ surpasses both $f_{P}$ and $f_{S}$ across the entire kinematic range. Furthermore, the derived uncertainties are sufficiently small to enable precise comparisons between theoretical predictions and both current and future experimental data. Given that $q^{2}=0$ represents the kinematic point most favorable for identifying the $B_{s}^{0}\rightarrow D_{s}^{*-}\mu^{+}\nu_{\mu}$ signal, this specific point is selected to compare our results with existing measurements. These findings align well with the latest experimental observations and indicate that $f_{T}(0)$ exceeds $200\,\text{KeV}$. Additionally, we examine the shape of the differential decay rate with respect to $q^{2}$, a property that remains underexplored experimentally, to facilitate the optimization of the signal-to-background ratio during data collection. Our results indicate that a peak in the $1/B_{s}^{0}\frac{d\Gamma}{dq^{2}}$ curve occurs near $q^{2}=5\,\text{GeV}^{2}$, suggesting that to maximize the integrated background within the feasible experimental range ($6\,\text{GeV}^{2}\le q^{2}\le q^{2}_{\text{max}}$), the center-of-mass energy should be as high as possible. Finally, we project that the precision required to constrain the hadronic form factors at the LHCb experiment within $2\%$ would necessitate a dataset amounting to approximately $30\,\text{ab}^{-1}$.

